NAMES.long:
The parallel flux-surface-averaged electric field at the plasma boundary is a separatrix scalar describing the net field-aligned electric drive at the interface between the closed-field-line plasma and the scrape-off layer. It is a magnetic-field-weighted surface average, distinct from [parallel_electric_field_at_plasma_boundary](name:parallel_electric_field_at_plasma_boundary), which represents the local projection of the electric field onto the unit magnetic-field direction.

Using a magnetic surface labeled by poloidal magnetic flux $\psi$, the boundary value is defined by

$$
\begin{aligned}
E_{\parallel,\mathrm{FSA},b}
&= \frac{\left\langle \mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{B}\right\rangle_{\psi_b}}{B_0}, \\
\left\langle f\right\rangle_{\psi_b}
&= \frac{\int_{\psi=\psi_b} f\, dA/|\nabla\psi|}{\int_{\psi=\psi_b} dA/|\nabla\psi|}
\end{aligned}
$$

where $E_{\parallel,\mathrm{FSA},b}$ denotes this boundary flux-surface-averaged quantity, $\mathbf{E}$ is the electric field in V m^-1, $\mathbf{B}$ is the magnetic field in T, $B_0$ is the signed reference toroidal magnetic field in T, and $\psi_b$ is the boundary value of $\psi$. In the averaging convention, $\psi$ is in Wb, $dA$ is a magnetic-surface area element in m^2, $\nabla\psi$ is in Wb m^-1, and $\langle f\rangle_{\psi_b}$ has the same unit as $f$. The toroidal direction is defined in the right-handed cylindrical $(R,\phi,Z)$ frame, where $R$ is major radius, $\phi$ is toroidal angle, and $Z$ is vertical height.

The quantity is computed by evaluating $\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{B}$ from an equilibrium magnetic field together with an electric field inferred from loop voltage, electrostatic-potential evolution, or a transport model, then applying the flux-surface average before division by $B_0$. Averaging the local projection $\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{B}/|\mathbf{B}|$ would give a different physical quantity. In diverted plasmas, care is needed near an X point because $|\nabla\psi|$ becomes small; practical analyzes should make clear whether a limiting closed surface or a regularized boundary average is used.

A useful order-of-magnitude scale is the loop-voltage electric-field magnitude $|V_\mathrm{loop}|/(2\pi R_0)$. In many quasistationary tokamak plasmas this scale is about 0.01-1 V m^-1; values close to zero can occur when inductive and electrostatic contributions nearly cancel, and transient or strongly driven edge conditions may exceed this range.
